# CHAPTER ONE
## A QUIET KIND OF EMPEROR
### Part Three of Three

---

He let them break at the half-hour mark.

Maerko had walked them through the seven sectors at a pace that Jaina, against her own will, found professional. He had a map. He pulled it up on the table's flat-projector with the side of his hand, the way you pulled up a maintenance manifest — no theatre, no zoom flourishes, just the borders he wanted them to look at. He named the worlds and the populations and the standing garrisons and the local tax revenues without consulting a single note. He named, when Jag pressed him on it, the two sectors he was *not* counting in his seven because their administration was provisional and he did not, in his own words, *take credit for work I'm still doing*.

He had asked them, at the half-hour, whether anyone needed a moment. He had said it the way a sergeant said it at the end of a brief: *anyone need a moment*. He had said it twice in case anyone wanted to take him up.

Jag had asked for ten minutes.

Maerko had said *take twenty, I'll take ten of those for myself.*

They had stood up. The aides had moved. Saska had appeared at Jaina's elbow with the small particular smile of a person who had been waiting to be useful.

"Jedi Solo," she said quietly. "Lord Maerko has asked me to offer you the use of a small room on this floor. Master Bel is invited as well. If you would like to see your former apprentice, this is a convenient time."

Jaina did not let her face change.

"He's offering me Mirella."

"He's offering you a conversation, Jedi Solo. With a Jedi healer present if you wish. The collar will be left at *off* for the duration. You will have privacy."

"How long?"

"Twenty minutes. He asks only that you do not attempt to remove her, and that you accept the room as it is."

"Audio?"

Saska did not insult her by hesitating.

"The room is audio-monitored, Jedi Solo. The recording will not be reviewed unless an incident occurs."

"Define incident."

"I would not presume."

Jaina looked at Master Bel. Bel inclined his head a fraction of a centimeter. *Go.*

"All right," she said.

---

Across the office, on the far side of the worktable, Maerko had drifted toward the windows.

Vale had come in.

Renad Vale was a year older than Maerko and looked five. He was lean where Maerko was broad, narrow-shouldered, narrow-faced, with the small dry mouth of a man who had spent thirty years saying *no* on behalf of someone else. He wore the same dark fatigues without insignia. The only thing on his person that suggested rank was the comlink at his belt, which was the small black model that did not exist on any quartermaster's list and which Maerko also wore.

They had served in the same garrison. They had been stationed in the same barracks block for four years. Vale had been the squad senior when Maerko was the squad junior, and the order had not, in any meaningful sense, reversed when Maerko stood up his junta; it had merely become formal. Vale still gave Maerko advice. Maerko still listened.

The hookah was lit.

Maerko did not smoke it often — not in front of company — but the company was thirty paces away and Saska had been instructed to keep them there, and the long brass pipe was a thing he kept lit on slow afternoons for the steadying of his hands. He drew on the hose once, slow, and let the smoke out through his nose in two thin plumes. The smell of it was a Tatooine blend, thick and faintly sweet and not quite tobacco; Vale had brought him a case of it two months ago and had not, so far, said where he had gotten it from.

Vale took the second hose. He drew on it. He held the smoke. He let it out.

"Three left," Vale said.

"I know."

"You let her have the apprentice."

"I let her have the apprentice."

"You think that's smart."

"I think," Maerko said, "that she walked in here ready to hate me on her uncle's behalf, and I would like, before she walks back out, for her to have looked her old student in the eye in a room where I am not in it."

"Why."

"Because the girl will lie for me."

Vale considered this. He drew on the hose again. He set it down.

"I don't think the girl will lie for you."

"She won't say what I tell her to say. She'll say what's true. Truth is, the girl is alive, healthy, and a pain in my ass. The Jedi will hear that out of her own mouth. That's worth more than anything I could say across this table."

"She'll see the collar."

"She'll see the collar. The collar's off. The collar's been off all morning."

"She'll see the scar."

"She'll see the scar. It happened in a battle. We didn't put it on her."

Vale's mouth did the tight thing it did when he was disagreeing without saying so.

"You like the girl," he said.

"I like the girl."

"Don't."

"I know."

"Don't, Jiyad."

Maerko looked at him. The frown had not moved. The eyes had.

"I'm aware," he said quietly. "I am aware, Renad. I would not be running this if I were not aware."

Vale nodded once.

They smoked.

A long silence. The kind of silence two men shared who had stood in a smelter-floor barracks together at nineteen, who had drunk bad caf on a parade ground at twenty-two, who had stood at the edge of a mass grave together at twenty-six and had not, either of them, said a word for the entire return trip. They did not need to fill silences. They had filled enough of them in their lives.

"The depot," Vale said eventually.

"Sealed."

"And the manifest."

"Gone."

"Hapan?"

"Slaver. Small-time. We'll get him."

"You want the team to pull off?"

"No. Let them work. I want to know who he was running them to."

Vale nodded. He drew on the hose. He held it. He let it out.

"She's going to ask for the apprentice back," he said.

"She is."

"You're going to give the apprentice back."

"Eventually."

"Now?"

"No," Maerko said. "Not now. Not yet. I need her to ask. I need her to ask twice. And I need her to know what she's trading."

He drew on the hose. The smoke came out through his nose. He did not smile, but the small thing the smoke did to the muscles at the corners of his eyes was, for an instant, almost gentle.

"Then I'll give her back," he said. "When it's time."

---

The small room two doors down was, by Maerko's own standards, almost beautiful.

It was square, eight meters by eight, with a single tall window that opened onto the inner courtyard with the tree. Pale stone walls. Dark wood at the wainscot. A low couch along one wall and two armchairs on the other and a small round table between them. Someone had set a pot of water on the table. Someone had set a plate of plain rusks beside it. There were no chairs in the room that did not have at least one cushion. The light was soft. The door, when it slid shut behind Jaina, made no sound.

Mirella was already there.

She was sitting on the couch.

She was sitting on it the way you sat on a couch you were not sure you were allowed to sit on, hands folded in her lap, ankles crossed, spine straight, the small careful posture of a person who had spent half a year being told to sit and stand and walk in specific ways and who had not, yet, unlearned them.

She was wearing the grey robes. The collar at her throat was thin and quiet. The light caught the small pale line of the scar along the angle of her jaw on the right.

She did not stand up when Jaina came in.

She did not stand up because, Jaina realized after a second, her knees were not steady enough to bear weight. Mirella's hands in her lap were perfectly still. Her face was perfectly still. But there was a fine constant tremor in her shoulders, the kind you got when your body had decided that the danger of crying was greater than the danger of being still and had compromised by shaking instead.

"Master," Mirella said.

Her voice was steady. Her voice was steady because she had decided, somewhere in the last three minutes, that it was going to be.

"Mirella."

Jaina had thought she would know what to do at this moment. She had thought about it for six months. She had run the version where she was angry, and the version where she was kind, and the version where she was professional and gathered information and did her job. She had run several other versions, in the small hours of the night, that she did not allow herself to remember in the day.

She did none of them.

She walked across the room. She sat down on the couch next to Mirella. She did not, at first, touch her. She set her hands on her own knees. She looked at the small round table.

"You look thin," she said.

A pause.

"I know," Mirella said.

"They feeding you."

"They feed me, Master."

"Enough?"

"Enough."

"Beatings."

"Three. The second week. Not since."

"For what."

"Not bowing at the right angle to a moff visiting from another sector. Not nodding back to a clerk when nodding back was, that month, the rule. Not setting a tea tray down at the correct edge of the table."

"Anything broken."

"A finger. The healer set it."

"Force-healing?"

"He let me. He doesn't always. He does when he wants me to keep working."

Jaina nodded once.

The door slid open. Master Bel stepped in. He inclined his head to Mirella. Mirella looked at him for a long moment, and the steadiness in her face cracked, only along one edge, only for half a second, before she fixed it back into place.

"Master Bel," she said. "I had thought you were —"

"I was. I'm not."

He crossed the room. He took the chair opposite her. He set his datapad on the table without looking at it. He looked at Mirella's face. He looked at her hands. He looked, for a long quiet moment, at the collar at her throat.

"May I," he said.

"Yes, Master."

He raised both hands. He held them just above her skin, not touching. He closed his eyes. The Force gathered around him in the small contained way it did when an old healer was working — no flare of light, no visible motion, only the room around them going slightly quieter the way a room went quieter when somebody important started thinking.

He worked his hands down. Throat. Shoulders. Ribs. He paused at the ribs.

"Two healed fractures here," he said, low. "Left side. Eight, ten weeks old."

"A guard," Mirella said. "He was new. He thought I was supposed to bow lower. He was reassigned."

"Reassigned where."

"I don't know, Master."

Bel's hands moved on. Down her back. Down her arms. He paused at her right wrist. He paused at her right knee. He paused, longest, at the small place at the base of her skull where the collar was anchored.

He opened his eyes.

"The collar is a clean install," he said quietly. "No tissue necrosis. Whoever fit it knew what he was doing. The Force-interrupt is on the inside of the band, but the trigger is remote. He has the only ring."

"He has the only ring."

"And he leaves it off."

"He leaves it off, Master. He calibrated it to a strength that lets me meditate. He says — he says he doesn't want a *broken* Jedi cleaning his tea."

Bel's mouth did the thin thing.

"How long do you sleep," he said.

"Five hours. Six on a good night. He doesn't keep me up. The schedule does. I get the early shift in the kitchens and the late shift in the office. Between them is when I sleep."

"You're losing muscle in your forearms."

"I know. I can't do the forms. The collar dampens the recovery. I do what I can in the cell at night."

"Bodyweight."

"Bodyweight. Some of the older sets. There's a corner of the room where I can do most of the Niman opener if I cheat the footwork."

"Don't cheat the footwork."

The smallest, smallest thing happened to Mirella's mouth. It was almost a smile. It was the answer of an apprentice to a teacher she had thought she would never hear again, given in the voice she had been answering teachers in since she was ten.

"Yes, Master."

Bel's hands moved on. He paused at her right shoulder.

"There's an old injury here."

"From the Hydian Way."

"It didn't heal cleanly."

"It didn't, Master. The medic on the carrier was — there wasn't time."

"I'll touch it. May I."

"Please, Master."

He set both palms on the shoulder, very lightly. He closed his eyes. The room went quieter again. The small contained gathering of the Force around him moved into Mirella's shoulder the way warm water moved into cold cloth, slow, gradual, finding every seam. Mirella did not move. She kept her eyes on her hands in her lap. A small line of muscle along her jaw worked, once, and stilled.

A minute passed. Two.

Bel opened his eyes. He took his hands away.

"Better."

"Thank you, Master."

"What else."

Mirella's hands tightened, fractionally, in her lap. The tremor in her shoulders was constant. She did not let it grow.

"He talks to me," she said. "Some nights."

"About what."

"About things he can't say to anyone else." A pause. "About his sister. About his mother, who's still alive on Vekkar Three, and who doesn't know what he is. About a boy in his squad from the smelter levels who died in front of him when the coup started, and whose name he says to himself sometimes when he thinks I'm not listening. About things I can't tell you and won't, Master, not because I'm protecting him but because they aren't useful to you and they would make you angrier than you can afford to be."

Jaina did not look at her.

Mirella, very softly: "He's lonely, Master."

Jaina shut her eyes for one second. She opened them.

"That's not your problem."

"I know."

"That's not your job."

"I know."

"He's used you for that."

"I know, Master. I'm telling you because you asked. I'm telling you because in twenty minutes I am going to be standing in his office pouring him tea and pretending that nothing was said in this room, and I want one person in the galaxy to know what was said in this room. So that if I die in this building, there's a record. So that the record is not the one *he* writes."

Jaina turned. She put her hand on Mirella's hand.

Mirella's hand was cold. Her fingers, after a moment, closed around Jaina's. They closed, then they tightened, then they tightened more, until Jaina felt the small bones of her own knuckles begin to grind. She did not pull away. Mirella did not let go. The tremor in her shoulders, finally, broke into a single hard shiver that ran the whole length of her body and stopped.

She did not cry. She did not, Jaina realized, allow herself to cry. The shiver was as close as she was going to let it come.

"We're going to get you out of here," Jaina said.

"I know, Master."

"Today."

"No, Master. Not today."

"Mirella —"

"Not today, Master. If you push him today, he won't move. He won't be pushed in front of his people. You know this. I know this. Trade. Trade slow. Trade for something he wants more than he wants me. Get me back next month, or the month after, or the month after that. I will be all right. I am all right. I have been all right for one hundred and seventy-eight days and I will be all right for thirty more. Please. Master. *Trade slow.*"

Jaina did not let her face change.

She did not let her face change because if she let her face change, the thing inside her chest that had been a small contained ember for six months was going to set the entire room on fire, and the room was monitored, and the man who was monitoring it would *hear* it, and she would not, in this building, in this afternoon, give that man the satisfaction.

She lifted Mirella's hand. She pressed the back of it, briefly, against her own forehead. She set it down.

"All right," she said.

Mirella drew breath. She let it out. She was, gradually, becoming the version of herself that could walk back into a corridor and pick up a tea tray.

"One more thing, Master," she said.

"Go."

"He's got a name for me."

"What."

"He calls me *kid*. Not in front of others. When it's just us. He's never used my name to my face. Not once. I think he can't."

Jaina did not say anything.

"It matters," Mirella said. "I don't know why. But it matters. Tell Master Skywalker. Tell him exactly that. He'll know what to do with it."

"I'll tell him."

"And Master."

"Yes."

"The ring. The one on his right hand. The small one. The plain band. That's the kill switch on this collar. He never takes it off. He never lets anybody else hold it. Not Vale. Not Saska. Nobody. Whoever you send to get me, the priority is the ring before the collar. If the ring leaves his finger before the collar comes off, the collar goes hot. It doesn't kill me. It does worse. He told me what it does on the second week and I have not, since, been brave enough to ask him whether he was joking."

"I'll remember."

"Don't write it down."

"I won't."

Master Bel, very softly: "Time."

---

They came back into the office to a man who was, by every visible sign, calmer than he had been when they had left.

Maerko was at the worktable. He had the hookah hose still in his right hand and he was, with his left, scribbling something on a flimsi pad with a stylus. He did not, this time, look up immediately. He let them sit. He let them settle. He let Saska set fresh cups in front of them. He set the stylus down and capped it.

"All right," he said. "Where we left off. Seven sectors. The Alliance recognizes de-facto. I want de-jure. Specifically, I want a signed treaty of recognition with the Galactic Alliance and the Imperial Remnant, jointly, witnessed by the Hapan Consortium and the Bothan Council. I'm not particular about the wording. I'm particular about the signatures."

Jag's chin came down a quarter centimeter. "Recognition of —"

"Recognition of the Maerkan Confederacy. That's what we're calling it. I didn't pick the name. The civic ministers did. I let them. They're proud of it."

Jaina said, "And the four contested."

"Two of the four I'll concede. Renkar and the Vellan Reach. Hand them back, you get to claim a win, the Senate gets to put it on a press sheet. Two I'm keeping. Tessell and Brae's Drop. They're already mine. The population voted. I'll send you the voting rolls if you like; they're not falsified, which is going to make somebody on your side unhappy."

"And the lanes."

"And the lanes," Maerko said. "All right. Lanes. The eastern terminus of the Hydian Way runs through three of my sectors. The Braxant Run terminates in mine. The Sanctuary Pipeline clips my border for about two parsecs. I want transit rights formalized in my name. I want toll collection on Braxant. I'll waive the toll on Hydian for any vessel carrying Alliance flag, because Hydian is the Alliance's artery and I'm not a damned fool. I want freedom to inspect any cargo that transits through my sectors flagged for the contested ordnance categories we discussed. I want — and this is the one that's going to make your ambassador grind his teeth, Lord Fel — a permanent diplomatic office in the GA Senate building on Denon."

He paused.

"And I want the stormtrooper program left alone."

Jag's mouth, very faintly, opened. "The —"

"The stormtrooper program. Mine. I'm not closing it. I'm not reforming it. I'm not pretending I'm going to. I was a trooper. I am a trooper. The men in the white armor outside this office are men I trained and men I will continue to train, and the day the Alliance asks me to dismantle them is the day this conversation ends. I'm telling you that now so we don't waste a half-hour on it later. Eat a dick."

There was a small pause.

"Pardon," Jag said politely.

"Sorry. Old Vekkar phrasing. Don't bother. The point stands."

Master Bel, against every visible bone in his body, made a small dry sound that might have been a laugh.

Maerko inclined his head to him. "Master."

"Lord Maerko."

"You laughed."

"I observed."

"What did you observe."

"I observed, Lord Maerko, that you are the most precisely impolite man I have met in forty years of diplomatic work."

"That a compliment?"

"It is a description."

Maerko's mouth pulled in the not-quite-smile. He inclined his head, half a centimeter, in something that was almost a bow.

"Now," he said. "Your turn. Lay it out. What does the Alliance want."

Jaina drew breath.

The side door hissed open.

---

The hit-team commander came in on his own boots.

He was a thin man, narrow at the shoulders, wearing the same dark fatigues as everyone else in the building. The trim on his collar was dark red. His hair was very short. His face was the face of a man who had been in a transport for the last seven hours and who had, twenty minutes before that, been in a building that had been on fire. He smelled of carbon scoring. The skin around his right eye was already starting to swell where something had hit him recently.

He came to the table. He stopped at the corner. He did not salute. He stood at the small respectful angle of an officer reporting in.

"Lord."

"Marek." Maerko set the stylus down. He looked, only for a second, faintly tired. "Go."

"Sir. The depot is secured. Civilian casualty count is zero. Hostages were recovered intact, all sixteen, families notified. Slaver fled prior to our entry through a secondary access. He was off-planet within thirty minutes of breach. We are tracking. The plans were not recovered. He had already passed them. Second-in-command was taken alive. He's downstairs."

Maerko nodded.

"Zero civilian casualties."

"Yes, lord."

"How."

"The slaver had the hostages in the rear holding bay. He bolted before our entry. He did not bring them with him. The bay door was unlocked."

"He left them."

"He left them, lord."

"Sloppy."

"Sloppy, lord."

"Good. Sixteen families. Names on my desk by tomorrow morning. The standard payment. Plus the school fees through tertiary." Maerko's eyes did not leave the commander's face. "The second-in-command. Downstairs where."

"Holding. East wing."

"Bring him up."

The commander blinked once, only once. He did not glance at the delegation.

"Up here, lord."

"Up here. The dining room. Set him at the small chair. The far end from the table. Bring me the meal. Bring the device. Bring Halver."

"Yes, lord."

"Now."

The commander turned. He was out the door in three steps.

Maerko did not, in the next five seconds, look at anyone. He turned the stylus end over end on the table once, slowly, with the pad of his finger. He set it down. He folded his hands together. He looked up.

"My apologies, again," he said. "I have to handle this. I'd like you to remain. I'd like you to remain because you should see how it is handled. I do not, as a matter of policy, conduct this kind of business in front of envoys. I am making an exception today because the manifest that was taken is a piece of state property that I would like, before this meeting ends, to know was *not* taken by you. I'm sure it wasn't. I would like, however, to be certain."

Voss, at the end of the table, did not speak. His face was very still. His hand on his teacup had not moved.

Master Bel said, evenly: "What kind of business."

"The kind, Master, that I would not have brought into your line of sight if I had a choice."

"You have a choice."

"I do not, today, no. I'd ask you to bear it."

Jag's face had gone very Chiss. It had gone very Chiss in the particular way that meant his New Republic surface had stepped out of the room and his Csilla-trained underneath had stepped forward and was, calmly and without protest, calculating the precise number of seconds it would take to put a chair between Jaina and the door if a chair had to be put there.

The side door hissed open.

Two men in matte black armor came in first. The armor was not the same as the white the troopers wore in the corridors. It was thicker at the joints, plated heavier, the visor a single horizontal slit. They were carrying a third man between them by the upper arms. The third man's feet were on the ground but only barely; his head hung. His face was unmarked but his hair was wet at the temples with sweat.

A third man in black armor came in behind them with a small case. He set the case on the side table. He opened it. Jaina, despite herself, looked.

Bolt cutters. Long ones. The heavy industrial kind, with the rubber-coated handles.

Behind him, a fourth man came in pushing a small wheeled cart. The cart held two covered plates and a bottle of wine, dust on the shoulder of the bottle in the way that meant it had not, that morning, been in the cellar. A clean napkin was folded along the cart's lower shelf.

The two men with the prisoner set him down in a low straight-backed chair at the far end of the room. They did not strap him. They stood, one on either side, hands at the prisoner's shoulders.

The prisoner lifted his head. He was Twi'lek. His lekku were lashed at the base with thin synth-cord. His eyes were red. His mouth was open and trembling. He saw, in order, Maerko, the bolt cutters, the four envoys at the table, and then Maerko again. The trembling in his mouth became a sound.

The sound was not, yet, a scream.

It would be soon.

---

Maerko sat down at his own end of the table. The cart was wheeled to his elbow. The plates were uncovered. A modest dinner: a grilled cut of meat, a side of grain, a small bowl of greens dressed in something pale. The wine was poured. The hookah hose was placed within reach.

He did not, at first, eat.

He looked down the length of the table at the prisoner. He picked up the wine. He sipped. He set it down.

"Your name," he said.

The Twi'lek's mouth worked.

"Halver Cinn, Lord. Halver Cinn."

"Halver. You're the boss's majordomo."

"I'm — yes, Lord. Yes."

"What did your boss take from my depot."

"I don't — Lord, I —"

"What did your boss take from my depot, Halver."

"He took — the plans, lord, he took the plans, I don't know which plans, I just know he —"

"What plans."

"I don't — I don't —"

"Halver."

The Twi'lek's voice cracked. "I swear on my mother, lord, I don't know which plans, he didn't tell us, he kept it locked in the —"

Maerko picked up his fork. He cut into the meat. He chewed.

"I believe you," he said pleasantly. "I believe you don't know which plans. I am going to ask you a different question, and I would like you to answer that one."

"Yes, lord. Yes, lord. Please."

"Where is he going."

"I don't — I don't know, lord, he didn't —"

"Halver."

"He never told us, lord, he never told the seconds, he kept it to himself and to his pilot, his pilot was —"

"His pilot."

"Drazek, lord, the Rodian, Drazek —"

"Drazek is dead, Halver. He died in the parking bay. The slaver shot him in the back when the slaver was running."

A small choked sound.

"I didn't know that, lord."

"I know you didn't. He had bad manners that way." Maerko cut another piece of meat. He chewed. He swallowed. He drew, idly, on the hookah hose. He let the smoke out through his nose. "Halver. Listen to me. I'm going to give you three more chances to tell me something useful, and then I'm going to start with the cutters. Where is he going."

"I don't —"

"Two."

"Lord, please, I —"

"One."

"Lord, *please*."

"Zero." Maerko set the fork down. He looked at the man with the cutters. He nodded.

The man with the cutters stepped forward.

The Twi'lek began to scream before anyone had touched him. The two men in black armor stripped the lower half of his robe with the practiced motion of men who had done this enough that they did not waste effort on it. The cutters opened.

Jaina's hand moved.

It moved an inch off the table. It did not move further, because Master Bel's hand had come, very gently, down on top of hers without ever looking, and his grip — the grip of a man who had once, on Yavin, held a temple gate against a wave of dark-jeedai by hand — was exactly as firm as a grip had to be.

Bel did not speak. His eyes were on Maerko.

Maerko was looking at the Twi'lek. He was looking at the Twi'lek without the eyes of a man enjoying something. He was looking at the Twi'lek the way a man looked at a part on a workbench he was about to apply tools to.

The cutters did not, in fact, close.

They did not close because the Twi'lek, when he saw them positioned, found, somewhere in the wreck of his nervous system, a new register of voice. He screamed. He screamed words. The words were not, at first, intelligible.

Maerko held up his right hand.

The cutters paused.

"Slower," Maerko said pleasantly.

The Twi'lek was crying. The Twi'lek was crying and screaming and shaking and the front of his trousers was darkening and the men in black armor were patient about all of these things.

"I know where he's going!" the Twi'lek howled. "I know where he's going, lord, I know, I know, I swear on the gods of my mother's house, please, please —"

"Slower."

"He — he was meeting the spooks, lord, the *spooks*, the Galactic Alliance spooks, the *intelligence* people, they were — they were going to pay him, they were going to pay him for the plans, they were going to —"

The room went very still.

It went still in a particular way. It went still the way a room went still when several different people in it had, simultaneously, decided not to breathe.

Maerko picked up his wine. He sipped. He set it down.

He turned his head.

He turned his head slowly. He did not turn his shoulders with it. He turned his head the way a turret turned its barrel, the rotation of a single piece of equipment finding a new angle of fire. His eyes finished their travel on the four faces at the other end of the table and stopped, in order, on each of them — Jag, Master Bel, Voss, Jaina — with the same small unhurried interrogation.

He stopped on Voss.

He held there.

The corner of his mouth pulled. It was the smallest movement. It was the not-quite-smile. He raised his right eyebrow about four millimeters.

He let the moment last.

He let it last longer than was comfortable.

He did not, at any point, say anything.

Voss, to his enormous credit, did not break. He set his cup down with deliberate care. He met Maerko's eye. He said, in a voice that was not quite his own but was, given the circumstances, an acceptable simulation:

"Lord Maerko. I do not know about this operation. Galactic Alliance Intelligence runs many programs in many sectors and I am not, despite the rank on my collar, briefed into most of them. I will, on returning to Denon, find out which directorate sanctioned this, and I will recommend in the strongest possible terms that the program be terminated and the responsible parties recalled. I'm telling you this on my own authority. I cannot promise that the directorate will agree."

A pause.

Jag, quietly: "On the record."

Voss: "On the record, Lord Fel."

Maerko held the look for two more full seconds.

Then he picked up his fork. He cut another piece of meat. He chewed.

"All right," he said.

He swallowed.

"I believe you, Colonel. I do. I believe you in the precise and limited sense that you, personally, did not know. I do not believe the Alliance did not know. I will be following up on that. Quietly. Through my own channels. I am telling you because, when my channels find what I believe they will find, I would like the conversation that follows to not be one in which I have to remind you that we had this exchange."

"Understood, Lord Maerko."

"Good." Maerko turned back to the prisoner. The prisoner was still in the chair. The men in the black armor were still in position. The cutters were still open in the hand of the third man. The Twi'lek was now, faintly, weeping. "Halver."

"Yes, lord. Yes."

"You've been useful."

"Yes, lord. Thank you, lord."

"I'm going to send you downstairs. You'll have a cell. You'll have a meal. You'll have a chance to talk to my interrogators tomorrow, and I expect them to find you talkative."

"Yes, lord. Yes, lord, thank you, lord, thank —"

"Lift him up."

The two men in the black armor lifted him. They did it gently. The lekku trailed. The man's legs barely held him, but they held. The man with the cutters folded the cutters and set them, almost tenderly, back into the case.

Maerko gestured at the lead guard, the taller of the two.

"A word."

The guard came over. Maerko leaned. He spoke into the man's ear for perhaps three seconds. The guard's helmet inclined a fraction. He turned. He went back to the prisoner. They walked him to the side door.

The side door hissed open.

The side door hissed closed.

---

Maerko picked up the wine. He sipped. He set it down. He picked up the fork. He cut the meat. He chewed.

"Apologies," he said. "Where were we."

Jag, evenly: "The Alliance was about to lay out its terms."

"Right. Yes. Floor's yours, Lord Fel. Take it."

Jag drew breath.

The window at the side of the room — the one that looked out over the inner courtyard with the tree — let in, very faintly, a sound.

It was a sound that traveled a long way down before it stopped.

It was, at first, a scream. The scream of a man who had been told, fifteen seconds ago, that he was being taken to a cell, and who had just discovered, on the threshold of a roof, that he was not. The scream was high. The scream was wordless. It traveled the four floors down the inside of the building's central airshaft without interruption. It ended, at the bottom, in a sound that the building's stone — Jaina noted, distantly, in the small clinical compartment of her mind that was still keeping notes — absorbed without echo.

Maerko did not look up.

Maerko, with his fork halfway to his mouth, smiled.

It was a small smile. It was, again, the not-quite-smile. It became, this time, almost the real thing. His shoulders moved in a single short jerk that was almost a laugh, and he turned his face down toward the plate to hide it, and when he looked up the not-quite-smile had been put away and the frown was back.

"Sorry," he said pleasantly. "Sometimes you have to. Lord Fel, the floor."

Jag did not, at first, speak.

He looked across the table at Jaina. Jaina did not look back. Jaina was looking at the cup of tea in front of her. Jaina was looking at the cup of tea because if she looked at anything else in this room, in this office, in this afternoon, she was going to do something that, four hours from now, she would not be able to undo.

Master Bel, very quietly, set his hand on her wrist a second time.

She did not look at him. She did not move.

Jag, after a long second, drew breath.

"Lord Maerko," he said. "The Galactic Alliance is prepared to discuss recognition of the Maerkan Confederacy on the following conditions. One. Permanent and verifiable abandonment of the Mandalorian-recovered fissionables. Independent inspection. Quarterly."

Maerko nodded, as if this had been expected.

"Counter," he said. "Bilateral. The Alliance pulls back its capacity to inspect mine when I get to inspect yours. Anaxes. Sluis Van. The Calamari yards. Equal access, equal frequency."

"That's not —"

"It's that or nothing. I'm not opening a door I can't watch through. I am — and I want this on the record because, Lord Fel, I am about to say something you can quote — *willing*, in principle, to surrender the older stockpile. I would rather not have it. It's a logistical headache and a moral one. I'll consider it. I will not consider it unilaterally."

Jag let two seconds go by. He set the tip of his stylus against the flimsi pad in front of him. He made a small note. The note, Jaina saw without meaning to, was a single word: *PROGRESS.*

"Two," Jag said. "The contested sectors. Renkar and the Vellan Reach you concede. Tessell and Brae's Drop the Alliance will recognize provisionally pending an independent plebiscite within twelve standard months. Hapan-observed. Bothan-counted. The result binds both sides."

"Twelve months is long."

"Twelve months is the time the Senate needs to swallow it."

Maerko thought about this.

"Eighteen."

"Fifteen."

"Fifteen. Hapan-observed."

"Hapan-observed. Bothan-counted."

"Bothan-counted." Maerko's mouth pulled. "I'm going to regret the Bothans. I always regret the Bothans. Three."

"Three. Transit. The Hydian Way provision stands. The Braxant toll —"

"Mine."

"The Braxant toll is yours, capped at the rate prevailing in 36 ABY, indexed to fuel."

"Indexed to *Hapan* fuel."

"Indexed to Hapan fuel."

"Sold."

"Four. The diplomatic office on Denon."

Maerko leaned forward, fractionally.

"This is the one you don't want to give me."

"This is the one we are prepared to give you, under conditions, because giving it to you is cheaper than not giving it to you. The conditions are these. The office occupies the standard envoy floor. Its head reports through the Hapan rotational delegation, not directly to your Confederacy. The office is closed to private functions and audits its guest list to the Senate Ethics Bureau. You get the address. You do not get the parties."

A pause.

Maerko's not-quite-smile did the small thing it did.

"You've thought about this."

"We've thought about it, Lord Maerko."

"You came in ready to give me this."

"We came in ready to give you what you would have taken anyway, in a form that lets us put a stamp on it. The Alliance is, despite many recent appearances, capable of foresight."

"All right. Sold. Five."

"Five. The stormtrooper program."

Maerko's eyebrows did not move.

"Five is mine. I told you. Eat a —"

"The Alliance is not asking for the program to be dismantled. The Alliance is asking for two things only. Inspection of conscription procedures in your sectors. And an end to the use of repurposed prisoners as auxiliaries in offensive operations."

A long pause.

Maerko set his fork down.

His face did not change in any way that anyone in the room would have called dramatic. The frown stayed where it was. The eyes stayed where they were. The hands stayed flat on the table. But the small invisible weight he kept under his shoulders, the gravity Mirella had taught Jaina, three floors above, how to feel, shifted. It tilted, half a degree, in their direction.

It was not a friendly tilt.

"The auxiliaries," he said.

"The auxiliaries, Lord Maerko."

"The contract auxiliaries. The six-month contracts. The vest brigades."

"Yes."

"You don't approve of those."

"The Alliance does not approve of those, Lord Maerko, no."

"They volunteer."

"They volunteer because the alternative is execution."

"And the alternative without my program is what, exactly. Execution. The same outcome with less paperwork." Maerko's voice had not risen. It had, if anything, gone quieter. The quiet was worse than the rise. "I have a process. The process gives them six months. Some of them survive. The ones who survive walk out clean. The records, by the way, are open. I'll show you the rolls. I will show you the *names* of the men who walked out, and the names of the worlds they walked out to, and the new ones the worlds gave them when they got there. The number is not large. It is not zero. The number you want me to put in its place is zero."

"The number we want is for the practice to end."

"That's a different sentence than the one you led with, Lord Fel."

"It is."

A pause.

Maerko looked, briefly, at Master Bel.

"Master."

"Lord Maerko."

"You think I should give this up."

"I think, Lord Maerko, that you should consider that the practice has a cost, and that the cost is not borne by the people who walk out."

"It's borne by the people who don't."

"It's borne, in a different way, by every man in your white armor who has stood next to one of them on a line. They know who is on their flank. They know what their flank is wearing."

Maerko held the look. He held it longer than any of his prior silences. He picked up the wine. He sipped. He set it down.

"I'll think about it," he said.

He did not say anything else for a long moment. The frown was set, the mouth straight. He turned the stylus over once.

"I won't promise. I'll think. That is more than I told the last envoy on the question, who left this room without a promise to think about anything, and who, the next morning, found that he was no longer the envoy. So you have, today, more than he had. Six. Go."

Jag did not flinch.

"Six. The Jedi apprentice Mirella Tarsh."

A small silence.

"Ah," Maerko said. "Six."

"Six."

"I knew this one was coming."

"You did."

"What do you want for her."

"The Alliance," Jag said, very carefully, "is prepared to accept the conditions discussed under headings one through five, including the diplomatic office on Denon and the framework for the plebiscite, against the return of the apprentice intact, with collar removed, today, in this building, to leave with this delegation when we leave."

Maerko looked at Jaina. He had not, until this moment, in any of the last twenty minutes, looked directly at Jaina.

Jaina looked back. She did not let her face change. She did not let her hands move. She did not, in any sense she was aware of, project anything.

Maerko held her eyes for a long count.

Then he turned back to Jag.

"No," he said pleasantly.

"Counter, Lord Maerko."

"Counter. You can have her in ninety standard days. I will return her at a location of your choosing, with the collar removed by my own physician under independent observation, in exchange for, in addition to the existing terms, recognition of my naval authority over the Braxant intercept corridor as it currently runs."

Jag did not, this time, write anything down.

"That's a hard ninety."

"It's a hard ninety. I'll review it on day forty-five for goodwill. I am not, today, in this room, in front of these envoys, handing her over, because if I hand her over in this room today she leaves with you and I look, to every officer in this building, like a man who can be moved by leverage. That is a thing I cannot afford to look like. I will hand her over, properly, in three months, on terms that read like a transfer and not a surrender, and you will tell your Chief of State that this was the best offer you got and you will be telling him the truth. Yes or no."

Jag glanced at Jaina.

Jaina, very slightly — a movement only Jag would have read — inclined her head.

*Yes.*

"Yes," Jag said. "Subject to drafting."

"Subject to drafting. Saska."

"Lord."

"Draft. By tonight. On my desk by twenty-two hundred."

"Lord."

He turned back to the delegation. The frown softened a fraction. It was, almost, gracious.

"That," he said, "is a productive afternoon. Better than I expected. Worse than you hoped. Which I'm told is the shape of a deal."

---

Outside the windows the sun was an hour past its highest. The shadow of the central spire had crawled across the courtyard and now reached the base of the tree. The smelter stacks on the horizon were still venting their clean white smoke in the long honest plumes of a working planet on a working afternoon.

The delegation talked. The warlord listened. The wine in his glass moved down, slow, an inch at a time.

Somewhere four floors below them, in a small inner court that did not have a tree in it, a sanitation crew that had been on standby for the entire afternoon began, without comment, to wash the stone.

Jaina, who could still feel the small bright point of Mirella alive and awake three floors below them, kept her hands flat on the table and did her job.

She would do it slow.

She would trade slow.

She would, in the end, get the girl back.

She would, in the end, get a great many things back from this man, in this building, before the matter that had begun this afternoon was finished, and she promised herself, with the small clean certainty she had learned from her uncle a long time ago, that she would remember this room.

She would remember the wine.

She would remember the cup.

She would remember the smile.

And, in time, she would remember the man, and what was owed.

---

*[End of Chapter One.]*
